In addition to forming a protective barrier, stratified squamous epithelial cells also play a part in the immune response. They can recognize pathogens, and in response, release chemical signals that attract immune cells to the site of potential infection. Moreover, they also carry out a variety of interactions with the immune system to ensure that invading pathogens cannot penetrate into deeper layers of the body.

